=== handler-side protocol: does call_runner block the kopf event loop? ===
controller image: jit-controller
kopf 1.44.6: event-loop thread 281473196614848; sync handler ran on thread 281473107816832; separate executor thread: True
default sync-handler executor: ThreadPoolExecutor max_workers=6
code reading: jit-controller/main.py handle_deployment is a synchronous def (line 129) and
resync_referenced_by is a synchronous @kopf.timer def (line 816); provision_infra -> call_runner
(line 352) is a plain blocking requests.post with timeout=600.
kopf/_core/actions/invocation.py line 134 executes a synchronous handler with
loop.run_in_executor(executor, real_fn), i.e. on a worker thread, not the event loop.
call_runner blocking verdict (derived from the probe above, rc=0): call_runner does not block
the kopf event loop - kopf offloads synchronous handlers to an executor thread, so the 30s resync
keeps ticking while an apply runs; asyncio.to_thread would be required only if S26 converts the
handler to async def.
caveat - the executor pool is the real ceiling, and this probe did not drive it there: kopf shares
that executor across every sync handler (handle_deployment, the 30s resync timer, the delete handler).
The pool's max_workers is printed above. The resync keeps ticking only while fewer than that many
applies are in flight; at the ceiling a 600s call_runner call queues the resync behind it, which is
the same stop the design describes, arriving through the pool rather than the event loop. S26 decides
whether to cap concurrency or move call_runner to asyncio.to_thread; this spike records the ceiling.

